Let's parse the comment:

```
// @@@ If the index is within valid range (0 to initial size inclusive) and the role is not null, then for every position from the index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null and the old element at that position was null, or it is equal to the old element at that position.
```

We need to express: If index in [0, old size] and role != null, then for each position i from index to old size-1? Wait "from the index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null and the old element at that position was null, or it is equal to the old element at that position."

We inserted role at index, shifting elements at positions >= index up by one. So after insertion, for each original position i (old list) from index to oldSize-1, the new element at i+1 equals old element at i (or both null). The comment says "element at the next position is either null and the old element at that position was null, or it is equal to the old element at that position." So new list's element at position i+1 is either null (and old element at i was null) or equals old element at i.

Thus we need to assert: (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . But need to handle null equality: Use == for null, and .equals for non-null. Since RoleUnresolved maybe object, we can use (this.get(i+1) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But spec uses "either null and old element null, or equal". Simpler: (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . However if this.get(i+1) is null, .equals would cause NPE, but we are in logical expression; we need to ensure we don't call .equals on null. Use conditional.

We can write: (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || (this.get(i+1) != null && this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But the specification may accept simpler.

Also we need to refer to role variable. In method signature, role parameter.

Also note list extends ArrayList<Object>, but get returns RoleUnresolved. We'll just use this.get(i) etc.

Thus final assert:

assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) && role != null) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); ( (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || (this.get(i+1) != null && this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) ) );

Make sure parentheses correct. Use && for conjunction.

Thus final answer in code block.

